A||||5||Leadfoot||Cheap trans fix...WOW!||||14||
Z||000000||Leadfoot||07-26-2001||09:37 AM||||As far as shifting went, my trans blew chunks.  The cable ends were pretty tight and I performed the selector adjustment several times with no shifting improvement to be had.  I also check if I needed to install a shim but the measurements were in specs.  I bought the Muncie select arm bushings from Rodney Dickman for $14.00 and all I can say is WOW!  Not only does the car shift much much better, the shift lever itself feels nice and tight.  Now I can go through the gears no problem.  ||||reg||14
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-26-2001||12:07 PM||||What is the select arm bushings that you are reffering to that come from Rodney?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||07-26-2001||02:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by starfighter007@msn.com:<BR><B>What is the select arm bushings that you are reffering to that come from Rodney?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It is on his transaxle page.  Fourth item from the top on the right hand side.  It is called the:<P>V6 Muncie 4-Speed Select Select Arm Rebuild Kit<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-26-2001||04:55 PM||||whats involved in mounting it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Brian87egotrip||07-26-2001||05:15 PM||||How hard is it to do? I'm not a good mechanic but I am good at breaking things. Brian||||reg||1
Z||000005||Leadfoot||07-26-2001||05:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Brian87egotrip:<BR><B>How hard is it to do? I'm not a good mechanic but I am good at breaking things. Brian</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The change took me about a half hour.  This is what I did:<P>(Note this is for the Muncie trans.)<P>1) Disconnect the shifter cables at the selector arm.  Make sure you don't loose the washers.<BR>2) For more work room I removed the clips that hold both cable to the trans and moved the cables out of the way.<BR>3) I then moved the selector arm so I could get to the pin.  MAKE SURE YOU HAVE YOUR E-BRAKES ON.<BR>4) Once the pin is removed just wiggle the arm out from its groove on the selector shaft. (this is where I wish I had some pics).<BR>5) Pop the old plastic bushings out and replace with the bronze ones.<BR>6) Install in reverse order but be prepared to spend some time to put the pin back through the selector arm.<BR>7) Adjust the trans selector. (see your hanyes, chilton or helms manual for this.) You are going to need 2 5/32 rods.  One should be about 4 inches and the other about 6 inches.  Drill bits will work too.<BR>8) Make sure your back-up lites still work.  If not turn the screw on the switch out until it does.<BR>9) Go for a test ride.<BR>10) Chug down a Mikes hard lemonade and pat yourself on the back.<BR>11) Get on the forum and look for more simple projects to do. <P>||||reg||1
